{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-81096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{MN}$ traversed from the point $M(0,1)$ to the point $N(3,3),$ then ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,dx-y\\,dy=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-81096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=j$ and $b=3\\,i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =j+t(3\\,i+3\\,j-j) \\\\  & =j+t(3\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =3t\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=3t$ and $y(t)=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=3\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,dx-y\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t)^{2}\\cdot 3-(1+2t)\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}27{t}^{2}-2-4t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[9{t}^{3}-2t-2{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[9(1)^{3}-2(1)-2(1)^{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-81096" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ed6a0b9e343556f26384f5d84edd57cd39a70a64b0b48ad56f2b55d7b810a20a"} {"question" "q-81096" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-81096" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "15"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "25"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-81096" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124663" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{MN}$ traversed from the point $M(-1,1)$ to the point $N(1,3),$ then ${\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+{x}^{2}\\,dy=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124663" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+j$ and $b=i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =-i+j+t(i+3\\,j-(-i+j)) \\\\  & =-i+j+t(2\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t-1$ and $y(t)=2t+1$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+1)^{2}\\cdot 2+(2t-1)^{2}\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+4t+1)+(4{t}^{2}-4t+1)\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}8{t}^{2}+2\\,dt \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{t}^{2}+1\\,dt \\\\  & =4{[\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4[\\frac{4}{3}+10]-4[0] \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124663" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ed6a0b9e343556f26384f5d84edd57cd39a70a64b0b48ad56f2b55d7b810a20a"} {"question" "q-124663" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124663" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "7"] ["math" "9"] ["math" "\\frac{20}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{25}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{28}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124663" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{28}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-39345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(1,2)$ to the point $B(3,4),$ then ${\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+y\\,dy=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-39345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+2\\,j$ and $b=3\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =i+2\\,j+t(3\\,i+4\\,j-(i+2\\,j)) \\\\  & =i+2\\,j+t(2\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =(1+2t)\\,i+(2+2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=1+2t$ and $y(t)=2+2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+y\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+2t)\\cdot 2+(2+2t)\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6+8t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[6t+4{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[6(1)+4(1)^{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-39345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ed6a0b9e343556f26384f5d84edd57cd39a70a64b0b48ad56f2b55d7b810a20a"} {"question" "q-39345" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-39345" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "18"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-39345" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(-1,2)$ to the point $B(1,0),$ then ${\\int }_{C}xy\\,dx+dy=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+2\\,j$ and $b=i.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =-i+2\\,j+t(i-(-i+2\\,j)) \\\\  & =-i+2\\,j+t(2\\,i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t-1$ and $y(t)=2-2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2\\,i-2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,dx+dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-1)(2-2t)(2)+(-2)\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-1)(2-2t)-1\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}-4{t}^{2}+6t-3\\,dt \\\\  & =2{[-\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+3{t}^{2}-3t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2[-\\frac{4}{3}+3-3]-2[0] \\\\  & =2[-\\frac{4}{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ed6a0b9e343556f26384f5d84edd57cd39a70a64b0b48ad56f2b55d7b810a20a"} {"question" "q-124652" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124652" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{8}{3}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124652" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{8}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124658" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(4,2),$ then ${\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,dx-{e}^{x+y}\\,dy=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124658" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0\\,i+0\\,j$ and $b=4\\,i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0\\,i+0\\,j+t(4\\,i+2\\,j-(0\\,i+0\\,j)) \\\\  & =4t\\,i+2t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=4t$ and $y(t)=2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=4\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,dx-{e}^{x+y}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-{e}^{x+y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x+y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{4t+2t}(4-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{6t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6t}}{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6(1)}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{6(0)}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6}-1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124658" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ed6a0b9e343556f26384f5d84edd57cd39a70a64b0b48ad56f2b55d7b810a20a"} {"question" "q-124658" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124658" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2{e}^{6}-1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{3{e}^{4}-1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{4}-2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{6}-1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{6}-3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124658" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{e}^{6}-1}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-81180" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=\\pi {x}^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(1,\\pi )$ to point $B(2,4\\pi ),$ then ${\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}})\\,dx+3x\\,dy=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-81180" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\pi {x}^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+\\pi {t}^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2\\pi t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}})\\,dx+3x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+3x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+3x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{2{t}^{2}})\\cdot 1+3t\\cdot 2\\pi t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+6\\pi {t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}1+6\\pi {t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & =[t+2\\pi {t}^{3}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[2+2\\pi (2)^{3}]-[1+2\\pi (1)^{3}] \\\\  & =[2+16\\pi ]-[1+2\\pi ] \\\\  & =1+14\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-81180" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ed6a0b9e343556f26384f5d84edd57cd39a70a64b0b48ad56f2b55d7b810a20a"} {"question" "q-81180" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-81180" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1+12\\pi "] ["math" "1+14\\pi "] ["math" "1+16\\pi "] ["math" "2+10\\pi "] ["math" "2+6\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-81180" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1+14\\pi "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y={x}^{3}$ traversed from the point $P(1,1)$ to point $Q(2,8),$ then ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{3}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+{t}^{3}\\,j,\\,t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+3{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}}+3t]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})}+3(2)]-[-\\frac{1}{2({1}^{2})}+3(1)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}+6+\\frac{1}{2}-3 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ed6a0b9e343556f26384f5d84edd57cd39a70a64b0b48ad56f2b55d7b810a20a"} {"question" "q-124668" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124668" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{19}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{27}{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{31}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124668" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{27}{8}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-80983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=\\sqrt{x}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,1),$ then  ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+y)\\,dx+(3x+2y)\\,dy=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-80983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\sqrt{x}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j={t}^{2}\\,i+t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2t\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+y)\\,dx+(3x+2y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(3x+2y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(3x+2y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({({t}^{2})}^{2}+t)\\cdot 2t+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+t)\\cdot 2t+3{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{t}^{5}+5{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5{t}^{3}}{3}+{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{(1)^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5(1)^{3}}{3}+(1)^{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}+1]-[0] \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-80983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ed6a0b9e343556f26384f5d84edd57cd39a70a64b0b48ad56f2b55d7b810a20a"} {"question" "q-80983" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-80983" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-80983" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-39679" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If the path $C$ is a part of the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ traversed from the point $P(0,0)$ to point $Q(1,1),$ then ${\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{xy}\\,dx+dy=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-39679" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j={t}^{3}\\,i+t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=3{t}^{2}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{xy}\\,dx+dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4y{e}^{xy}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4y{e}^{xy}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4t\\cdot {e}^{{t}^{3}\\cdot t}\\cdot 3{t}^{2}+1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12{t}^{3}{e}^{{t}^{4}}+1\\,dt \\\\  & ={[3{e}^{{t}^{4}}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[3{e}^{(1)^{4}}+(1)]-[3{e}^{(0)^{4}}+(0)] \\\\  & =[3e+1]-[3] \\\\  & =3e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-39679" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ed6a0b9e343556f26384f5d84edd57cd39a70a64b0b48ad56f2b55d7b810a20a"} {"question" "q-39679" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-39679" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2e-3"] ["math" "3e+4"] ["math" "3e-2"] ["math" "4e-3"] ["math" "5e+4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-39679" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3e-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-90871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=\\pi {x}^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,\\pi ),$ then ${\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})\\,dx+4{x}^{2}\\,dy=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-90871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\pi {x}^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+\\pi {t}^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2\\pi t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})\\,dx+4{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+4{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+4{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}})\\cdot 1+4{t}^{2}\\cdot 2\\pi t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +8\\pi {t}^{3}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}8\\pi {t}^{3}-1\\,dt \\\\  & =[2\\pi {t}^{4}-t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[2\\pi (1)^{4}-1]-[0] \\\\  & =2\\pi -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-90871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ed6a0b9e343556f26384f5d84edd57cd39a70a64b0b48ad56f2b55d7b810a20a"} {"question" "q-90871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-90871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi "] ["math" "2\\pi +1"] ["math" "2\\pi -1"] ["math" "4\\pi +3"] ["math" "\\pi -1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-90871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi -1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-81192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{y\\,dy-x\\,dx}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(0,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-81192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y\\,dy-x\\,dx}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{y\\cdot {y}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t-x\\cdot {x}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{y\\cdot {y}^{′}-x\\cdot {x}^{′}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[-1-1] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-81192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ed6a0b9e343556f26384f5d84edd57cd39a70a64b0b48ad56f2b55d7b810a20a"} {"question" "q-81192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-81192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-81192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(x-2y)\\,dx+2x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(0,3)$ to the point $B(0,-3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $3,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-2y)\\,dx+2x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(x-2y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+2x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}((x-2y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+2x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6{sin}^{2}⁡t+6{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}6-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ed6a0b9e343556f26384f5d84edd57cd39a70a64b0b48ad56f2b55d7b810a20a"} {"question" "q-124655" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124655" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}3-{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}6-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}6{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124655" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}6-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{2}{y}\\,dx+y{e}^{x}\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(1,0)$ to the point $B(0,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $2,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{2}{y}\\,dx+y{e}^{x}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2}{y}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+y{e}^{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{2}{y}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+y{e}^{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-1\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ed6a0b9e343556f26384f5d84edd57cd39a70a64b0b48ad56f2b55d7b810a20a"} {"question" "q-124657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+1\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-1\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-2\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-1\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}({e}^{x}+y)\\,dx-x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(0,\\sqrt{3})$ to the point $B(-\\sqrt{3},0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({e}^{x}+y)\\,dx-x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }({e}^{x}+y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(({e}^{x}+y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}-x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }({e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-3{sin}^{2}⁡t-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+3\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ed6a0b9e343556f26384f5d84edd57cd39a70a64b0b48ad56f2b55d7b810a20a"} {"question" "q-124653" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124653" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+3\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+3\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+1\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124653" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+3\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx-{x}^{2}\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(1,0)$ to the point $B(0,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx-{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{y}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({y}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}-{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-{sin}^{3}⁡t-{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t+{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ed6a0b9e343556f26384f5d84edd57cd39a70a64b0b48ad56f2b55d7b810a20a"} {"question" "q-124597" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124597" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t+{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "q-124597" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t+{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/state.json" "file_sha256" "70665e0560373c39334eed94f8b8cd407e5ab63adb575228190c0f14aa0f2a35"} {"question" "e-9515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overline{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(1, 1),$ calculate the line integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C x^2y\\,\\mathrm{d}x + xy\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/example-9515.json" "file_sha256" "418840ca46981644c584fdf82a2593efde44bd729beacd5b8e96ff53306662b1"} {"question" "e-9515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a} = 0\\,\\mathbf{i} + 0\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = 1\\,\\mathbf{i} + 1\\,\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =t\\,i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ So, we have that $x(t) = t$ and $y(t) = t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,dx+xy\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\cdot t\\cdot 1+t\\cdot t\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/example-9515.json" "file_sha256" "418840ca46981644c584fdf82a2593efde44bd729beacd5b8e96ff53306662b1"} {"question" "e-7038" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=x^2$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(1,1),$ evaluate the line integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x + (x-y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/example-7038.json" "file_sha256" "20cf2cd219a135f7eef5fc628b8b4dddf1a77e126ac92757a834c0fed5a1f0a5"} {"question" "e-7038" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y = x^2.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(x-y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+{t}^{2})\\cdot 1+(t-{t}^{2})\\cdot 2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t+3{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+{t}^{3}-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+1-\\frac{1}{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/example-7038.json" "file_sha256" "20cf2cd219a135f7eef5fc628b8b4dddf1a77e126ac92757a834c0fed5a1f0a5"} {"question" "e-7035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/assets/7a6e67435aab40d5bf3630c66f79241008357e1549b1c77b4c0bdfea2b453883.png)\n\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C \\dfrac{y\\,\\mathrm{d}x - x\\,\\mathrm{d}y}{x^2+y^2},$ where $C$ is a part of the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $\\left(0,2\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/example-7035.json" "file_sha256" "26242ff0a9b68156bbe14affa6c55eeadba01b865f222f2fc8d3c3d86994fbb0"} {"question" "e-7035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that a parametrization for the given path is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{′}(t)\\,i+{y}^{′}(t)\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y\\,dx-x\\,dy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{y\\cdot {x}^{′}-x\\cdot {y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}},dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{-4{sin}^{2}⁡t-4{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{-4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}{4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,dt \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,dt \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059731/example-7035.json" "file_sha256" "26242ff0a9b68156bbe14affa6c55eeadba01b865f222f2fc8d3c3d86994fbb0"}] "usage" {"e-7038" [] "q-81192" [] "e-9515" [] "e-7035" [] "q-81096" [] "q-124597" []} "basis" 1904 "retractions" [[17592186180219 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $MN^(―)$ traversed from the point $M(0, 1)$ to the point $N(3, 3)$, then $∫_(C)x^{2}dx - ydy =$"] [17592186180222 :question/problem "Evaluate $∫_(C)\\frac{ydy - xdx}{x^{2} + y^{2}}$, where $C$ is part of the circle $x^{2} + y^{2} = 1$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(1, 0)$ to the point $(0, 1)$."] [17592186179939 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $∫_(C)y^{2}dx - x^{2}dy$, where $C$ is part of the circle $x^{2} + y^{2} = 1$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(1, 0)$ to the point $B(0, 1)$."] [17592186179520 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overline{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(1, 1)$, calculate the line integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C x^2y\\,\\mathrm{d}x + xy\\,\\mathrm{d}y$."] [17592186179520 :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = 0\\,\\mathbf{i} + 0\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = 1\\,\\mathbf{i} + 1\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= a + t(b - a) \\\\\n&= ti + tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n0 \\le t \\le 1\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $x(t) = t$ and $y(t) = t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j = i + j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)x^{2}ydx + xydy &= ∫_{0}^{1}x^{2}y\\frac{dx}{dt}dt + xy\\frac{dy}{dt}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(x^{2}y\\frac{dx}{dt} + xy\\frac{dy}{dt})dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}t^{2} \\cdot t \\cdot 1 + t \\cdot t \\cdot 1dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}t^{3} + t^{2}dt \\\\\n&= [\\frac{t^{4}}{4} + \\frac{t^{3}}{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179460 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=x^2$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(1,1)$, evaluate the line integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x + (x-y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y$."] [17592186179460 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y = x^2$. As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t) = x(t)i + y(t)j = ti + t^{2}j, t ∈ [0, 1]\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j = i + 2tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)(x + y)dx + (x - y)dy &= ∫_{0}^{1}(x + y)\\frac{dx}{dt}dt + (x - y)\\frac{dy}{dt}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}((x + y)\\frac{dx}{dt} + (x - y)\\frac{dy}{dt})dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(t + t^{2}) \\cdot 1 + (t - t^{2}) \\cdot 2tdt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}t + 3t^{2} - 2t^{3}dt \\\\\n&= [\\frac{t^{2}}{2} + t^{3} - \\frac{t^{4}}{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{2} + 1 - \\frac{1}{2}] - [0] \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179459 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C \\dfrac{y\\,\\mathrm{d}x - x\\,\\mathrm{d}y}{x^2+y^2}$, where $C$ is a part of the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $\\left(0,2\\right)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3703/Source/Images/3b681a72da2d77baebf38cb87698a572.png)"] [17592186179459 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that a parametrization for the given path is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= x(t)i + y(t)j = 2\\cos ti + 2\\sin tj, t ∈ [0, \\frac{π}{2}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= x^{′}(t)i + y^{′}(t)j =-2\\sin ti + 2\\cos tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)\\frac{ydx - xdy}{x^{2} + y^{2}} &= ∫_{0}^{π/2}\\frac{y \\cdot x^{′} - x \\cdot y^{′}}{x^{2} + y^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/2}((2\\sin t)(-2\\sin t) - (2\\cos t)(2\\cos t))/((2\\cos t)^{2} + (2\\sin t)^{2}), dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/2}(-4\\sin^{2} t - 4\\cos^{2} t)/(4(\\cos^{2} t + \\sin^{2} t))dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/2}(-4(\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t))/(4(\\cos^{2} t + \\sin^{2} t))dt \\\\\n&= - ∫_{0}^{π/2}dt \\\\\n&=-\\frac{π}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186177635 17592186177636 17592186177637 17592186177638 17592186177639 17592186179190 17592186179191 17592186179192 17592186179193 17592186179194 17592186179208 17592186179209 17592186179210 17592186179211 17592186179212] "immutable_fields" [[17592186177634 :answer-field/correct] [17592186177634 :answer-field/id] [17592186177634 :answer-field/key] [17592186177634 :answer-field/type] [17592186179189 :answer-field/correct] [17592186179189 :answer-field/id] [17592186179189 :answer-field/key] [17592186179189 :answer-field/type] [17592186179207 :answer-field/correct] [17592186179207 :answer-field/id] [17592186179207 :answer-field/key] [17592186179207 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
